CONTENT
Playbook ID: RD-003. Title: "RD-003: Plain Frontend Requests Reach the Frontend Surface".
What it verifies: a frontend request written in everyday words, with no file path and no jargon, returns a compiled route whose targets include sk-code-frontend. The risk it guards: the frontend vocabulary once held only specialist phrases, so ordinary requests returned defer and the frontend references never loaded.
Realistic user request: a developer asks for an ordinary frontend fix in plain words.
Exact prompt (the first of the set, used for the "Exact prompt" block and the "Prompt:" line):
fix the responsive grid layout on the pricing page
The full prompt set, which section 3 replays, one per line:
fix the responsive grid layout on the pricing page
make the navbar sticky with css
build a modal with a focus trap in React
improve LCP on the landing page
add a dark mode toggle to the site
the button text has poor contrast
style the form inputs and error messages
write a css animation for the loading spinner
implement the checkout page from the figma file
center a div vertically
add i18n support with rtl languages
optimize images and lazy loading on the homepage
make the table scroll horizontally on mobile
Preconditions: node is callable, and `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-guard.cjs --json` reports the sk-code hub as fresh.
Exact Command Sequence: 1. save the 13 prompts, one per line, to /tmp/skc-RD003-prompts.txt. 2. run: while IFS= read -r p; do node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt "$p"; done < /tmp/skc-RD003-prompts.txt > /tmp/skc-RD003-routes.txt  3. run: grep -c '"workflowMode":"sk-code-frontend"' /tmp/skc-RD003-routes.txt
Expected signals: 13 route lines, each with "action":"route" and a target whose workflowMode is sk-code-frontend. The count command prints 13.
PASS iff the count is 13. FAIL iff any prompt returns defer or a route without sk-code-frontend.
Evidence: /tmp/skc-RD003-routes.txt
Failure triage: 1. Find the failing prompt's line in the routes file. 2. If the guard reports a stale manifest, the router is serving the legacy path, so run the refresh command the guard prints and replay. 3. Otherwise add a phrase of two or more words from the failing prompt to the matching frontend class in hub-router.json and to the matching row of INTENT_SIGNALS in both ROUTER.md files. 4. Never edit a prompt to make it pass, and never add a single-word keyword.

VERIFY (run from the repository root):
grep -c '^## [1-5]\. ' .skilled/skills/sk-code/manual-testing-playbook/routing-disambiguation/frontend-reach.md
Expected output: 5
